[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Handling StaleElementReferenceException & Timeouts in Selenium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building automated tests with Selenium, encountering StaleElementReferenceException and TimeoutException is a common hurdle. These exceptions arise due to the dynamic nature of modern web applications where the Document Object Model (DOM) is constantly updating."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding StaleElementReferenceException"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A StaleElementReferenceException occurs when Selenium attempts to interact with an element that no longer exists on the current web page. This usually happens because the page has been refreshed, navigated away, or JavaScript has dynamically rebuilt the DOM element, rendering the previous object reference obsolete."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategies to Handle Stale Elements"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Re-locate the element: Instead of storing the WebElement in a global variable, fetch it right before performing the action."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement explicit waits: Wait for the element to stabilize or re-appear using ExpectedConditions."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use custom retry logic: Wrap the interaction in a loop to catch the exception and retry locating the element."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Retry Mechanism for Stale Elements"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public WebElement findElementWithRetry(By locator, int maxRetries) {
    int attempts = 0;
    while (attempts < maxRetries) {
        try {
            return driver.findElement(locator);
        } catch (StaleElementReferenceException e) {
            attempts++;
            if (attempts == maxRetries) throw e;
        }
    }
    return null;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Timeouts in Selenium"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Timeouts occur when Selenium waits for a specific condition (like page load, script execution, or element visibility) to be met within a predefined timeframe. If the condition fails, a TimeoutException is thrown."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Waits to Manage Timeouts"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implicit Wait: Tells the driver to poll the DOM for a certain amount of time when trying to find an element."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Explicit Wait: Used to wait for a precise condition before proceeding, making tests more reliable."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Implementing Explicit Waits"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
WebElement element = wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("submitButton"))
);
element.click();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Robust Automation"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid mixing implicit and unpredictable explicit waits to prevent unexpected delayed timeouts."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage ExpectedConditions.refreshed() when dealing with elements known to frequently undergo DOM updates."}]
